<ArticleLayout slug="campaign-adaptation-cost-per-market" ctaLabel="Get a market range for your rollout" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">Search this and you get media budgets, because the production question has no agreed vocabulary. "Adaptation" covers a resize and a reshoot with local talent, which are not the same purchase and should never share a line on a budget.</p>

  <h2>How much does campaign adaptation cost per market?</h2>

  <p>There is no per-market average, and no source publishes one. What exists is a single published rate card, one worked example of a ladder, and a great deal of confident writing with no denominator behind it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Across six phrasings a buyer would actually type, forty-nine results returned one page that puts a price on an adaptation and says what the price includes. No page in the set defines adaptation as a ladder of levels with a cost attached to each level.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search result review conducted 10 September 2026, six queries, every result classified by page type and by whether it publishes a cost.</p>

  <p>One page in forty-nine. And the shape of who is writing explains the rest.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Roughly four in five results for these queries are published by a party selling the service, the software or the media being described. Not one is a buyer-side benchmark, a trade body instrument or a survey with a stated sample.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search result review conducted 10 September 2026, forty of forty-nine results counted by hand from the six result sets.</p>

  <p>So this page does the only useful thing available: it defines the unit, prices what can be priced, and is explicit about where the arithmetic stops.</p>

  <h2>What is a toolkit adaptation?</h2>

  <p>The words buyers use are versioning, toolkit adaptation, master and adapt, and localization, and they are used interchangeably for jobs that differ by more than an order of magnitude in effort.</p>

  <p>A useful unit needs three numbers, not one. A rollout is one master, across N markets, in M formats. Multiply those and you get slots, which is the thing being bought. One master across twelve markets in six formats is seventy-two slots, and a quote that does not state its slot count is not comparable to anything.</p>

  <p>But slots alone still hide the important variable, which is how deep the change goes in each slot. That is the ladder nobody publishes, so here is one.</p>

  <h2>The five levels, defined here because nobody else defines them</h2>

  <p><strong>This ladder is this page's own definition.</strong> No trade body publishes it, no rate card uses these names, and it is offered because the category has no shared vocabulary and a buyer needs one to compare quotes. Use it as a scoping instrument, not as an industry standard.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Level</th>
          <th scope="col">What changes</th>
          <th scope="col">What stays</th>
          <th scope="col">What it scales with</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Resize</th>
          <td>Frame and safe areas only</td>
          <td>Every creative element</td>
          <td>Formats, not markets</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Retitle</th>
          <td>On-screen text and end card</td>
          <td>Footage, voice, music</td>
          <td>Languages, not markets</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Recut</th>
          <td>Structure, duration, pacing</td>
          <td>Captured material</td>
          <td>Formats and platforms</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Recast</th>
          <td>Voice, talent, or on-screen product</td>
          <td>Concept and structure</td>
          <td>Markets, and rights</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Reshoot</th>
          <td>New capture in market</td>
          <td>The idea only</td>
          <td>Markets, fully</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Read the last column. Only two of the five levels scale with the number of markets at all, which is the single most useful thing on this page. A rollout priced as though every slot were a reshoot is priced wrong, and so is one budgeted as though every slot were a resize.</p>

  <h2>What one published card actually prices</h2>

  <p>There is exactly one, and it is worth reading closely because of what it names.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">One published adaptation rate card in this category starts at 1,350 euro excluding VAT for a 15-second commercial adaptation, and states exactly what that buys: a new voice-over, new titles, a new packshot, an audio mix and mastering.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a published commercial adaptation rate card, read 10 September 2026. One card, one duration, one inclusion list, and no publication date printed on the page.</p>

  <p>That inclusion list is the valuable part, more than the number. Voice, titles, packshot, mix, master. In the ladder above, that is a recast without new capture, and knowing which level a price covers is what makes two quotes comparable at all.</p>

  <p>Note also what sits outside it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same card bills broadcast master files separately, at 65 euro per file, and quotes anything outside the inclusion list, logo changes, dubbing an actor, recording a new product line, only after scoping it.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the same published adaptation rate card, read 10 September 2026. Published prices read directly from the seller's own page.</p>

  <p>Deliverable files billed per file, and everything genuinely creative quoted separately. That is the standard shape, and it means the headline number covers the middle of the job and neither end.</p>

  <p>For the lighter levels of the ladder, the relevant published rates come from different categories entirely, which is itself a finding: no single card prices the whole ladder.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published rate cards in this category put the floor for ecommerce image editing between roughly $0.25 and $1.20 an image, with one card adding a fixed platform fee of about $95 a month on top.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, collected September 2026, two independent cards read directly and compared, no vendor named.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published studio rate cards put a standard product image at $39 to $50, a model or lifestyle image at about $95, a short video clip at about $93 and a single UGC video at about $89, with editing add-ons around $49 each.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, collected September 2026, two independent cards read directly, prices quoted as each card states them.</p>

  <p>Language work is priced on a different basis again, and the reason matters.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published rate cards in the translation category put professional English to Simplified Chinese at roughly $0.08 to $0.18 per source word, and price transcreation per hour or per project instead, because source word count does not track the creative work.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages collected across the category, September 2026, no company named. Rate cards only, no survey and no method published behind the band.</p>

  <p>Transcreation is priced by time because rewriting a line so it lands in another culture has no relationship to how many words the original had. Any adaptation budget that prices copy by word count has assumed the cheap version of the job.</p>

  <p>These bands come from different cards, different categories and different currencies. They are not additive and this page does not add them, because a sum built from euro, dollar and per-word units would read as a quote while meaning nothing.</p>

  <h2>Why no benchmark exists to check a quote against</h2>

  <p>Two structural reasons, and neither is anyone behaving badly.</p>

  <p>The organizations best placed to publish rate benchmarks put them behind membership, and the membership condition is that you contribute your own rates first.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The UK advertiser trade body's 2025 creative and media agency hourly rate benchmarks are released only to members who contribute their own rates, in aggregated form.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's own published access condition, read September 2026.</p>

  <p>That is a reasonable way to run a benchmark and a complete barrier to a buyer trying to sanity-check one quote. It also means the numbers that do exist cannot be cited, argued with or checked.</p>

  <p>Meanwhile the largest dataset anywhere near this question measures something else entirely.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The largest creative dataset published in this category, 578,750 creatives across 6,015 brands and $1.29 billion of ad spend, reports weekly creative volume and expected hit rate. It reports no cost per asset in any currency.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a creative analytics benchmark in this category, 2026, platform data, period covered not stated by the publisher.</p>

  <p>More than half a million creatives and no cost figure. It can tell you how many versions a brand runs and how many work. It cannot tell you what one costs, because the platform holding that data never sees an invoice. Volume data and cost data live in different places, and nobody has joined them.</p>

  <h2>Does adaptation cost scale linearly with markets?</h2>

  <p>No, and the places it breaks are predictable enough to plan around.</p>

  <p><strong>Markets and languages are not the same count.</strong> A twelve-market rollout is rarely twelve languages. If eight languages cover twelve markets, the language work is two thirds of what a naive model assumes, while the legal and platform work stays at twelve. Those two lines move independently and should be budgeted separately.</p>

  <p>Run it on the one published price, keeping it in its own currency. Twelve adaptations at 1,350 euro is 16,200 euro. Eight is 10,800 euro. That single distinction, markets against languages, is a 5,400 euro difference on the same brief, before anyone negotiates anything.</p>

  <p><strong>Deliverable files scale with markets times formats, not with markets.</strong> At 65 euro a broadcast master, twenty-four files is 1,560 euro. That line is small per unit and grows fastest, because it multiplies twice.</p>

  <p><strong>Rights do not scale smoothly at all.</strong> They step.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under the US performers' union commercials agreement effective 1 April 2025, alternate scenes shot for one commercial in the same session are not treated as new commercials, while an edit or lift outside the defined parameters creates a new commercial and new fees.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the union commercials agreement effective April 2025, as read by two independent law firms. The union's own rate documents are not machine readable.</p>

  <p>That is the non-linear breakpoint in the whole model. Recuts inside the defined parameters are cheap versioning. One recut outside them is a new commercial with new fees, and the cost jumps rather than climbs. It is also invisible in every production quote, because the industry's own bid form does not price it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US commercial production trade body's standard bid form itemizes a production across sections A through P, from prep crew to breakout talent, and carries no line at all for media usage payments.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's own free bid form, updated January 2023, downloaded and read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p><strong>Legal and platform load steps by market too.</strong> Some markets add a compliance layer that has nothing to do with creative effort and everything to do with what must be recorded, labeled and disclosed. China is the clearest case, and the article on what it costs to localize a campaign for China covers that stack rather than repeating it here.</p>

  <h2>A toolkit at twelve markets, line by line</h2>

  <p>One master, twelve markets, six formats, eight languages. Each line is quoted in the currency its own source publishes, and no total appears, because these come from different cards with different units and adding them would produce a number that looks like a quote and means nothing.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
