  <p>Two rows carry no published basis at all. Recut and master production are central to any rollout and neither has a rate anyone publishes, which is worth knowing before you accept a quote that prices them confidently.</p>

  <p>The pattern in the "scales with" column is the deliverable here. Copy that column into your own budget, count each driver once, and you will have a model that survives contact with a real rollout better than any per-market average would.</p>

  <h2>What drives adaptation cost up</h2>

  <p><strong>Treating every slot as the deepest level.</strong> The commonest and most expensive error. Scope each slot to a ladder level before pricing anything.</p>

  <p><strong>Counting markets where you should count languages,</strong> or the reverse. They drive different lines and the gap between them is real money.</p>

  <p><strong>Discovering rights late.</strong> A recut that steps outside the agreed parameters converts a versioning job into a new commercial. Settle the parameters when the master is made, not when the cutdowns are due.</p>

  <p><strong>Pricing copy by the word.</strong> Transcreation is priced by time for a reason, and a word-count budget has silently assumed translation instead.</p>

  <p><strong>Leaving deliverable files out of the count.</strong> They multiply twice and are the line most often forgotten, being individually trivial.</p>

  <p>There is one more constraint worth naming, which is the budget the whole thing comes out of.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Production accounted for an average of 19 percent of total marketing budgets, against 24 percent before the pandemic.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025, a survey of 50 global brands across more than ten sectors. It publishes no absolute cost per asset.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Nearly two thirds of those brands expect to increase social and short-form video production, and one in three now builds production relationships directly with digital platforms.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: global advertiser trade body content production research, March 2025. The same 50-brand survey.</p>

  <p>More formats out of a smaller share. That is the pressure making the ladder worth using: the only way those two facts coexist is if most slots are being delivered at the cheaper levels, deliberately rather than by accident.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/pricing">pricing page</a> sets out how a rollout is scoped against a written spec, the <a href="/solutions/agencies">agencies solutions page</a> covers the roster shape this usually sits inside, and the <a href="/resources/production-cost">production cost estimator</a> will take your own slot count and return a market range rather than a quote.</p>

  <p>Reviewed September 2026. Every figure is quoted in the currency its source publishes and nothing on this page is converted or averaged across currencies.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about adaptation cost</h2>

  <h3>How much does campaign adaptation cost per market?</h3>

  <p>No per-market average exists and no source publishes one. Across six buyer phrasings, one page in forty-nine puts a price on an adaptation and says what it includes: 1,350 euro excluding VAT for a 15-second commercial covering new voice-over, titles, packshot, mix and master, with deliverable files billed separately at 65 euro each.</p>

  <h3>What is a toolkit adaptation?</h3>

  <p>One master reworked across N markets and M formats. The useful unit is slots, markets times formats, so one master across twelve markets in six formats is seventy-two slots. A quote that does not state its slot count cannot be compared to another quote.</p>

  <h3>Does adaptation cost scale linearly with markets?</h3>

  <p>No. Resizes scale with formats, retitling scales with languages, and only recasting and reshooting scale with markets. Rights step rather than scale: a recut inside agreed parameters is versioning, while one outside them creates a new commercial with new fees under the US performers agreement.</p>

  <h3>What drives adaptation cost up?</h3>

  <p>Scoping every slot at the deepest level, confusing market count with language count, discovering rights parameters after the master is made, pricing transcreation by source word when the category prices it by time, and forgetting deliverable masters, which multiply across markets and formats and are billed per file.</p>

  <h3>What is the difference between versioning and adaptation?</h3>

  <p>In practice the words are used interchangeably, which is the problem. This page splits the work into five levels, resize, retitle, recut, recast and reshoot, so that a quote can be matched to a level. That ladder is this page's own definition rather than an industry standard.</p>
